<Base title="The place" current="/en/the-place/" lang="en" alt="/le-lieu/">
  <p class="eyebrow">The place</p>
  <h1>81, route 263</h1>
  <p class="lede">A 104-acre land in Saint-Fortunat, in the Appalachians, "2 h 15 from Montreal, 1 h 15 from Quebec City". What was there, and what the Agorians built on it.</p>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>According to the ad the collective posted in 2014: "on the Agora's land there's a small river, a good stream, a small lake, an insulated building 180 feet by 40, a barn and stable, a house, a regional hiking trail, four spruce plantations due to be cut into boards for building, 2,500 maples ready for a sugar bush, a mountain and summit, and the land is 104 acres". The 180 feet of the ad is the part of the building that was heated in winter; in all, it was 300 feet long.</p>
    <p>And, from the 2012 description: "The Agora's main building comprises lodges, bedrooms, a kitchen, showers and a big common room with a stage, all heated with wood for perfect comfort during the winter season. On our land there are four treehouses, two stages in the forest, the Cherry Island, the barn and a yurt, all equipped with electricity and wireless internet access. It is also possible to walk a magnificent hiking trail that leads to St-Fortunat's lookout tower at the top of the mountain."</p>
    <blockquote><p>A 180-foot section was heated in winter with the wood we cut in the forest, in the one big wood stove we had. Despite the minus 20 outside, and even after a week without heating, the concrete on the floor kept warming the place, it held the heat so well. Everything was watched very, very closely to make sure of everyone's safety, and of the children who came to marvel at the place. Given the Peter Pan atmosphere, the place was lit up by pirates who came to tell us their life stories.</p><cite>Christian, 2026</cite></blockquote>
  </div>

  <div class="facts">
    <div class="fact"><strong>The bunker</strong><span>the former pig barn, 300 × 40 feet: lodges, bedrooms, kitchen, showers, big hall with a stage, wood stove</span></div>
    <div class="fact"><strong>The Cherry Island</strong><span>the island of cherries, in the middle of the stream: cabin, summer kitchen, fire, beach, hammock</span></div>
    <div class="fact"><strong>The Neverland</strong><span>the round-log stage of 2013, with its decks in the trees and its walkways</span></div>
    <div class="fact"><strong>5 treehouses</strong><span>l'Ascension, la Riveraine, the CubeHouse and the others, with electricity and Wi-Fi</span></div>
    <div class="fact"><strong>The mandala tipi</strong><span>25 feet, two floors, "for your acrobatics"</span></div>
    <div class="fact"><strong>The yurt</strong><span>sleeps eight, electricity and Wi-Fi, at the end of the spruce alley</span></div>
    <div class="fact"><strong>The barn</strong><span>twenty hammocks, "warmer and drier than outside"</span></div>
    <div class="fact"><strong>The lookout</strong><span>the tower at the top of the mountain, at the end of the regional hiking trail</span></div>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>L'AgorA's campground, in 2012</h2>
    <p>"Please note that all accommodations have electrical outlets, lights and Wi-Fi. A yurt for rent at $100 for the three nights of the weekend, which can sleep 8, with electricity and Wi-Fi. Two treehouses for rent at $60 each, which can sleep 4, with a hay bed to keep warm (efficient, and generates heat in all seasons). The barn, which can hold 20 hammocks and is warmer and drier than outside, can house 25 people with beds or air mattresses. The Agora itself has 5 lodges for rent at $75 which can house 4 each, next to the wood stove." Camping, $10 a night per person, "including access to both sound systems".</p>
    <h2>The plans</h2>
  </div>
